DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppnnmmttooppaallmm  reads  a  PNM image as input, from Standard Input or _p_n_m_f_i_l_e
       and produces a Palm Bitmap as output.

       Palm Bitmap files are either grayscale files with 1, 2, or 4  bits  per
       pixel,  or  mapped  color files with 8 bit per pixel, or a direct color
       file with 16 bits per pixel, and ppnnmmttooppaallmm  chooses  this  color  depth
       based on the maxval and number of colors in the input, unless you spec‐
       ify a depth (bits per pixel) with --ddeepptthh.  You can also specify a maxi‐
       mum  depth  with  --mmaaxxddeepptthh  to partially constrain ppnnmmttooppaallmm's choice.
       Input files must have an appropriate number and set of colors  for  the
       selected output constraints.

       This  often means that you should run the PNM image through ppnnmmqquuaanntt or
       ppnnmmrreemmaapp before you pass it to ppnnmmttooppaallmm.  Netpbm  comes  with  several
       colormap  files  you  can use with ppnnmmrreemmaapp for this purpose.  They are
       _p_a_l_m_g_r_a_y_2_._m_a_p (4 shades of gray for a depth of  2),  _p_a_l_m_g_r_a_y_4_._m_a_p  (16
       shades of gray for a depth of 4), and _p_a_l_m_c_o_l_o_r_8_._m_a_p (232 colors in de‐
       fault Palm colormap).  In a standard Netpbm installation, these are  in
       the  Netpbm  data directory, and you can find the Netpbm data directory
       with a nneettppbbmm--ccoonnffiigg ----ddaattaaddiirr shell command.

       Example:

         pnmremap myimage.ppm \
                  -mapfile=$(netpbm-config --datadir)/palmgray2.map \
         | pnmtopalm -depth=2 >myimage.palm



       Compressed Palm Bitmap files, at least the ones ppnnmmttooppaallmm knows how  to
       create,  cannot have more than 8 bits per pixel.  ppnnmmttooppaallmm defaults to
       8 bits per pixel if you specify a compressed output.  You  can  specify
       the number of bits per pixel explicitly with --ddeepptthh.  --mmaaxxddeepptthh has the
       same effect as --ddeepptthh.  If you specify more than 8 bits per pixel  with
       either of these, ppnnmmttooppaallmm fails.


   PPaallmm BBiittmmaapp VVeerrssiioonn
       ppnnmmttooppaallmm  generates a Version 0, 1, 2, or 3 Palm Bitmap.  It generates
       the oldest (lowest) version it can for the given image and the  options
       you specify.



       •      If  you  specify  a density (--ddeennssiittyy option) higher than "low,"
              the version is at least 3.


       •      If you specify transparency (--ttrraannssppaarreenntt option)  or  any  com‐
              pression, the version is at least 2.


       •      If you specify a custom colormap (--ccoolloorrmmaapp option), the version
              is at least 1.


       •      If the image has more than one bit per pixel, the version is  at
              least 1.  The image has more than one bit per pixel if you spec‐
              ify it with --ddeepptthh or if you let it default and  the  image  has
              more than two colors (or shades of gray).



       All  releases  of Palm OS can read a Version 0 bitmap.  Palm OS 3.0 and
       later can read a Version 1 bitmap.  Palm OS 3.5 and later  can  read  a
       Version  2 bitmap.  To read a Version 3 bitmap, you need Palm OS Garnet
       or a handheld running the High Density Display Feature Set.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppnnmmttooppaallmm recognizes the
       following command line options:



       --vveerrbboossee
              Display the format of the output file.


       --ddeepptthh==_N
              Produce a file of depth _N, where _N must be either 1, 2, 4, 8, or
              16.   Because  the default Palm 8-bit colormap is not grayscale,
              if the input is a grayscale or monochrome image, the output will
              never  be  more  than  4  bits deep, regardless of the specified
              depth.  Note that 8-bit color works  only  in  PalmOS  3.5  (and
              higher),  and  16-bit direct color works only in PalmOS 4.0 (and
              higher).  However, the 16-bit direct color format is  also  com‐
              patible  with  the various PalmOS 3.x versions used in the Hand‐
              spring Visor, so these images may also work in that device.


       --mmaaxxddeepptthh==_N
              Produce a file of minimal depth, but in any  case  less  than  _N
              bits  wide.   If  you  specify 16-bit, the output will always be
              16-bit direct color.


       --ooffffsseett
              Set the nneexxttDDeepptthhOOffffsseett field in the palm file header  to  indi‐
              cate  the  end  of  the  file  (and pad the end of the file to 4
              bytes, since nneexxttDDeepptthhOOffffsseett can point only  to  4  byte  bound‐
              aries).

              A  palm  image  file can contain multiple renditions of the same
              image, with different color depths, so a viewer can  choose  one
              appropriate  for  the  display.  The nneexxttDDeepptthhOOffffsseett field tells
              where in the stream the next rendition begins.

              ppnnmmttooppaallmm creates a file that contains only one image,  but  you
              can  separately concatenate multiple one-image files to create a
              multi-image file.  If you do that, you'll need to use --ooffffsseett so
              that the resulting concatenation is a correct stream.

              By default (if you don't specify --ooffffsseett), ppnnmmttooppaallmm generates a
              nneexxttDDeepptthhOOffffsseett field that says there is no following image (and
              does not add any padding after the image).

              Version  3  Palm  Bitmaps actually have a nneexxttBBiittmmaappOOffffsseett field
              instead of the nneexxttDDeepptthhOOffffsseett.  The foregoing applies to which‐
              ever is relevant.

              The  --ooffffsseett option was new in Netpbm 10.26 (January 2005).  Be‐
              fore that, ppnnmmttooppaallmm always set  the  nneexxttDDeepptthhOOffffsseett  field  to
              "none."

              Before  Netpbm 10.27 (March 2005), you cannot use --ooffffsseett if you
              create a compressed raster (because ppnnmmttooppaallmm isn't smart enough
              to  be  able to know the size of the image at the time it writes
              the header).  You also cannot use it with 16 bit color depth  or
              with the --ccoolloorrmmaapp option, for much the same reason.


       --wwiitthhdduummmmyy
              This  option tells ppnnmmttooppaallmm to put in the stream, after the im‐
              age, a dummy image header to introduce subsequent  high  density
              images.

              This  dummy image header is a special sequence specified in Palm
              Bitmap specifications.  It looks to an older Palm Bitmap  inter‐
              preter like an invalid image header, so such an interpreter will
              stop reading the stream there.  But a  new  Palm  Bitmap  inter‐
              preter  recognizes it for what it is (just something to choke an
              old interpreter) and skips over it.  Presumably, you will add to
              the stream after this high density images which would confuse an
              older interpreter.

              If you specify --wwiitthhdduummmmyy, you must also specify --ooffffsseett,  since
              it doesn't make any sense otherwise.

              --wwiitthhdduummmmyy was new in Netpbm 10.27 (March 2005).


       --ccoolloorrmmaapp
              Build a custom colormap and include it in the output file.  This
              is not recommended by Palm, for efficiency reasons.   Otherwise,
              ppnnmmttooppaallmm uses the default Palm colormap for color output.


       --ttrraannssppaarreenntt==_c_o_l_o_r_s_p_e_c
              Marks _o_n_e particular color as fully transparent.

              _c_o_l_o_r_s_p_e_c   is   as   described   for   the   argument   of  the
              ppnnmm__ppaarrsseeccoolloorr(())                 library                 routine
              ⟨libnetpbm_image.html#colorname⟩ .

              Transparency works only on Palm OS 3.5 and higher.


       --ssccaannlliinnee__ccoommpprreessssiioonn
              Specifies that the output Palm bitmap will use the Palm scanline
              compression scheme.  Scanline compression works only in Palm  OS
              2.0 and higher.


       --rrllee__ccoommpprreessssiioonn
              Specifies that the output Palm bitmap will use the Palm RLE com‐
              pression scheme.  RLE compression works only with  Palm  OS  3.5
              and higher.


       --ppaacckkbbiittss__ccoommpprreessssiioonn
              Specifies that the output Palm bitmap will use the Palm packbits
              compression scheme.  Packbits compression works only  with  Palm
              OS 4.0 and higher.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.27 (March 2005).


       --ddeennssiittyy=_N
              This specifies the Palm Bitmap density.  The density is a number
              that is proportional to the resolution  the  image  should  have
              when displayed.  The proportionality factor is up to whatever is
              doing the displaying, but it's helpful to think of these numbers
              as being pixels per inch.  The allowable values are:



       •      72

       •      108

       •      144

       •      216

       •      288


              This  option  was  new  in  Netpbm  10.27 (March 2005).  Earlier
              Netpbm could not generate Version 3 Palm Bitmaps, so  there  was
              no such thing as density.




